# Dependencies And Parallel Work

## Overview

A Flow graph gets dependencies from result references and explicit `needs`
entries. Source order does not create a dependency.

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install The Package

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-beta.11"}])
```

## Define A Diamond

```elixir
defmodule FlowDependencies.Echo do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_dependencies_echo"

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end

defmodule FlowDependencies.Diamond do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "flow_dependencies_diamond"

  flow do
    step "left",
      action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
      params: %{side: :left, value: input(:value)}

    step "right",
      action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
      params: %{side: :right, value: input(:value)}

    step "join",
      action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
      params: %{
        left: result("left", :value),
        right: result("right", :value)
      }

    output(%{joined: result("join")})
  end
end
```

`left` and `right` are independent. `join` depends on both because its
parameter expression reads both results.

```elixir
{:ok, dependencies} =
  FlowDependencies.Diamond.flow()
  |> Jido.Flow.dependencies()

dependencies
```

## Use Needs For Control Only

`needs:` orders work without copying predecessor output into the next Action.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
step "publish",
  action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
  params: %{status: :published},
  needs: ["audit", "approve"]
```

Use a result reference when the Action needs data. Use `needs` only when the
dependency is an author control decision.

Jido keeps the three forms separate:

```elixir
%{
  needs: ["audit"],
  references: ["render"],
  effective: ["audit", "render"]
}
```

## Execute Independent Work

Run the Flow with a Runic concurrency limit:

```elixir
{:ok, %{joined: %{left: 4, right: 4}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    FlowDependencies.Diamond,
    %{value: 4},
    %{},
    max_concurrency: 2
  )
```

Runic derives the ready frontier from graph dependencies. `left` and `right`
can run together. `join` becomes ready only after both results exist. The
option changes scheduling capacity. It does not change the graph.

For low-level inspection, compile the Flow and use Runic's public preparation
API:

```elixir
workflow = Jido.Exec.compile!(FlowDependencies.Diamond)
workflow = Runic.Workflow.plan_eagerly(workflow, %{value: 4})
{workflow, runnables} = Runic.Workflow.prepare_for_dispatch(workflow)
Enum.map(runnables, & &1.node.name)
```
